    "tree": {
      "sha": "884eea793ce092981a0d02605953392126e7c2f6",
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/jphacks/tkb_2615/git/trees/884eea793ce092981a0d02605953392126e7c2f6"
    },
    "message": "Take a Checkpoint capture's entities and owned namespaces from their Rust rows\n\nThe rows write the canonical JSON of each entity and namespace, and a row nothing\nwrote keeps its bytes object. So a capture encodes and parses none of it, and a restore\ncheck rebuilds only the rows that changed since the check that showed them equal. Its world is bare,\nso it holds no rows of its own. An old history entry is known unchanged by the objects\nit holds instead of being read and compared with its document (HistoryPins), and what a\nCheckpoint holds and its hashes do not change.\n\nAlso count a decision's observed state in Rust, read each row's privacy and token input\nonce in the index process, and take an event row's key from the bytes sent to it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
